  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="kicker">About</div>
      <h1>One family, one idea: quantum computing is more fun hands-on</h1>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <em>Fun with Quantum</em> is the front door to a family of open-source quantum outreach
          projects that grew over years of talks, workshops, and conference booths: quantum{' '}<a href="/play/">games</a>{' '}you
          play in the browser, quantum computer models you{' '}<a href="/build/">3D-print or build from LEGO</a>, and hands-on
          ways to{' '}<a href="/learn/">learn and teach</a>. Everything is open source and tested on real audiences, from
          school classes to IEEE conferences.
        </p>
        <p>
          The family is built by <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/JanLahmann" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jan-R.
          Lahmann</a> and the RasQberry community. Contributions are welcome in every repository —
          start at <a href="https://github.com/JanLahmann" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/JanLahmann</a>.
          Questions, ideas and bug reports: open an issue in the{' '}<a href="https://github.com/JanLahmann/Fun-with-Quantum/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fun-with-Quantum repository</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
        <p>
          This site has no accounts, no sign-up and no cookies. The browser games run entirely in your
          browser; nothing you do in them is sent anywhere, apart from the anonymous counts below and
          the questions you choose to ask the explainer.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Page counts.</strong> We count page views and a few named clicks (for example a notebook
            launch or a finished game round) with{' '}<a href="https://umami.is" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Umami</a>, a
            cookieless analytics service. Umami sets no cookies and does not identify you; it records the
            page, the site you came from, and coarse facts such as browser, operating system, device type,
            language and country.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The explainer.</strong> Under some games you can ask questions in a chat (“Ask the
            explainer”); the RasQberry helper on rasqberry.org works the same way and sends the page you are on. Only when you send a question, it goes together with the visible game state
            (chapter, moves, score), the explanation level you picked and the page language through our server at Cloudflare to Anthropic,
            whose Claude model writes the answer (see Anthropic's{' '}<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy policy</a>).
            We store the question, the answer, the game state and your thumbs up or down for 30 days to
            improve the explainer, then delete them. We store no IP address and no cookie with them; to
            limit requests, the server counts questions per day under a pseudonym derived from your IP
            address that changes every day. The level you pick (Kids, Normal, With math) is remembered only in
            your own browser. Please don't enter personal data.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Hosting.</strong> The site is served by GitHub Pages; GitHub processes your IP address to
            deliver it (see GitHub's{' '}<a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy statement</a>).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Other sites.</strong> The 3D model on the homepage loads its viewer from cdn.jsdelivr.net
            and the model from quantego.org. Notebook buttons open QuBins or mybinder.org, and other links
            lead to the family's own sites and to IBM — each with its own privacy policy.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
